    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Traditionalist theologians often see modern changes in marriage and family definitions as a challenge to foundational moral and theological principles. Their primary criticism lies in the belief that these institutions, originally designed to serve moral and religious functions, are now increasingly centered around individual desires and societal norms rather than divine guidance or communal values. This shift is viewed as undermining the intrinsic purposes of marriage and family, such as procreation, stabilizing society, and nurturing spiritual growth within a religious framework. Traditionalists contend that prioritizing the subjective preferences of individuals threatens to erode the moral fabric of society by neglecting these conscious, higher purposes.",
